-- Effects: effect declaration and well-known effect kinds
--
-- Each effect kind is a sort parameterized by what it ranges over:
--   sort Modify { sort T = ? }   — the effect MARKER; its argument names the
--                                  mutated RESOURCE, i.e. a PLACE, not a type.
--
-- A `Modify` target is a PLACE — a parameter (`Modify[c]`), the result binder
-- (`Modify[result]`), or a field path off one (`Modify[c.contents]`). It is
-- never a TYPE: kernel-language.md §5.6 reads `Env` as a partial map from
-- resource NAMES to terms, so `Modify[X]` says the operation may update
-- `Env(X)`, and a type in that slot names no slot at all. `Modify[T = c]` is
-- the same thing spelled with the binding named. Enforced at load by
-- `check_modify_targets` (kb/typing.rs); WI-20260823-39AD2 records why the
-- refusal and not a comparison.
--
-- The Modify marker carries only WHICH resource is mutated. What writes a
-- resource is its sort's own operations (`Cell.set`); a sort with no operations
-- of its own has nothing that writes it (proposal 037 §3.5, §4.1).
--
-- Effect kinds are registered by `provides Effect[T = Kind]` — written in the
-- kind's own body, or in a `namespace <Kind>` secondary entry (059) where the
-- kind has no body to write it in. That is the ONE spelling since
-- WI-20260917-S8JYF retired the namespace-level `fact Effect[T = Kind[?]]` one
-- (058 §4): a `fact` is an ordinary fact and nothing else, so nothing derives a
-- carrier from a binding value any more. CHECKED since WI-20260823-VM3YB: an
-- effect row naming an unregistered sort is a load error
-- (`typing::check_effect_registration`).
-- Before that the registration was documented here and asked at no site, so a
-- misspelled label was a silent new effect and every one of these lines was inert.
-- The kernel checks effect declarations; interpretation is provided
-- by the realization layer (host-language, unsafe).
-- See kernel-language.md §5.5 (Effects).
--
-- Design principle: effects declare what is NOT visible from parameters.
-- Currently implemented:
--   Modify[target] — mutates a parameter (non-obvious from signature)
--   Error          — can fail with an untyped error
--   Error[type]    — can fail with a typed error
--
-- NO AMBIENT RESOURCES. A slot is named by a PARAMETER, `result`, or a field path
-- off one, and a resource is threaded to the operation that writes it:
--
--   operation write(c: Cell[V = Int64], n: Int64) -> Unit effects Modify[c] = Cell.set(c, n)
--
-- `Modify[counter]` over a nullary constructor `counter` is the constructor's type, as
-- a constructor is in every bracket, and is refused; so is a zero-argument operation's
-- result (`Modify[slot]`), which no call can write. Kernel-language.md §5.6.

namespace anthill.prelude

  -- Effect: meta-sort for effect registration.
  -- fact Effect[T = Modify[?]] means "Modify[target] for any target is a valid effect."
  -- The `[?]` reads well and is INERT: what a registration records is the KIND, its base
  -- sort, so `[T = Modify]` registers exactly the same thing. A label is judged on the
  -- sort it names, never on the arguments it applies (kernel-language.md §5.5).
  sort Effect {
    sort T = ?
  }

  -- Modifiable: marks a type as admitting a `Modify` over a PLACE OF THAT TYPE in
  -- effect rows — Cell's `provides Modifiable[T = Self]` is what makes `Modify[c]`
  -- with `c: Cell` admissible. (`Modifiable`'s parameter is a TYPE; a `Modify` target
  -- never is — see the file header.)
  -- Proposal 037 Rule 8: a `Modify` applies only where the place's type admits it.
  -- A base sort admits it through its `Modifiable` claim, a secondary sort through
  -- containment. A base sort has a default implementation, its host realization,
  -- which the load holds to the sort's `@[host_implemented]` declarations.
  --
  -- Enforced by `check_modify_targets` (WI-20260823-TR8CF): `Modify[pattern]` on a
  -- `pattern: String` parameter is a load error naming `Modifiable`. A type is
  -- modifiable when a provision names it, when it `provides` a sort that is, when a
  -- type argument or entity field of it is, or when it is a type parameter.
  sort Modifiable {
    sort T = ?
  }

  -- Well-known effect kinds.
  -- Each sort defines its operations — the "pseudo API" that the effect provides.

  -- Modify[p]: the effect MARKER, keyed by the mutated resource — a PLACE.
  -- This is what effect rows carry (`effects Modify[c]` / `effects Modify[s]`);
  -- it says only WHICH resource is mutated, not what value it holds, and not
  -- its type. A TYPE in the slot (`Modify[T]`) is a load error — see the file
  -- header and kernel-language.md §5.6.
  sort Modify
    sort T = ?

    -- registed as en effect kind
    -- TODO: in modify should be expression, which modified.  We have no syntax for it.
    -- TODO: why Modify[?], not Modify[T]
    provides Effect[T = Modify[?]]
  end

  -- Error[T]: failure carrying a payload of type T, and the boundary that
  -- catches it. `raise` and `reify` are Filinski's pair at `M = Result`
  -- (proposal 047 §3, 027.4): `raise(x)` REFLECTS `err(x)` into direct-style
  -- code, `reify` REIFIES a computation back into a `Result`. `Result.reflect`
  -- closes the loop the other way, which is what makes this a monad LAYER
  -- rather than a try/catch — the caught value is ordinary data.
  sort Error
    import anthill.prelude.{Nothing, Result}
    import anthill.reflect.{TypePattern}
    sort T = ?
    provides Effect[T = Error[?]]
    operation raise(error: T) -> Nothing effects Error[T] @[host_implemented]

    -- THE BOUNDARY. Runs `body`; a raise inside it aborts HERE and becomes
    -- `err(payload)`, a normal return becomes `ok(value)`.
    --
    -- `Error[T1]` IN THE PARAMETER ROW IS THE DISCHARGE, not decoration. A
    -- handler's whole type is "the label present on the body side, absent from
    -- the result" (WI-329) — there is no `handle` keyword and no per-effect
    -- typer rule, so deleting the label would let `Rho` absorb it and the effect
    -- would escape to the caller. It is also the only occurrence that names the
    -- payload, so `T1` — and hence `Result[E = T1]` — comes from nowhere else.
    --
    -- `T1`, an operation parameter, NOT the sort's `T`: the payload belongs to
    -- this call and is inferred from the body's row, so a caller writes nothing.
    -- (Naming it `T` would shadow the sort's and give `Error.reify[T = …]` two
    -- targets — proposal 058 §4.2 rule 1.)
    --
    -- Body-less on purpose: the boundary is a FRAME, and the interpreter
    -- installs it by symbol (`Interpreter::enter_reify_boundary`). It cannot be
    -- a builtin — a builtin returns a value and cannot enter a closure.
    --
    -- A rule body calls it with a lambda:
    -- `rule r(?x) :- Error.reify(lambda () -> f(), ?x)` answers the `Result`. An
    -- operation named bare there is its zero-argument call, made before the
    -- boundary exists, so the thunk is written as a lambda. A raise the boundary
    -- declines is reported as a fault of the goal, and the thunk's other effects
    -- are those of any higher-order call in a rule body.
    --
    -- IT CATCHES ITS OWN PAYLOAD AND LETS THE REST PAST. A body declaring two
    -- `Error` labels reified at one of them discharges only that one; the other
    -- stays in the CALLER's row, which the typer already requires it to declare.
    --
    -- `requires TypePattern[T = T1]` IS WHAT MAKES THAT DECIDABLE (proposal 071
    -- §1.4). A raise reaching the boundary is caught when the `admits` of the
    -- pattern this clause puts in the call's requirement channel answers yes for
    -- the raised value's type; the boundary calls that API and reads no dictionary
    -- by position. Until 071's stage (d) a raise carries no type, and the type
    -- asked about is the raised value's head sort. Before WI-20260921-28TAT there
    -- was no evidence at all, and where the type-argument channel could not ground
    -- `T1` the boundary silently caught every payload — swallowing a label its own
    -- row had left to its caller. A call whose `T1` has no `TypePattern` evidence
    -- is refused at load. A call whose evidence is taken from its arguments when
    -- it runs — a host's, or a generic operation's that a rule body calls —
    -- refuses to run where they do not fix `T1`.
    --
    -- SO A GENERIC BOUNDARY DECLARES IT. An operation reifying at its OWN type
    -- parameter writes `requires TypePattern[T = P]`, and its callers supply the
    -- evidence — the ordinary requirement route, one hop per generic level. The
    -- one row of `TypePattern` answers wherever `T1` has a `TypeValue`, and a tuple
    -- payload, or a sort with one among its type arguments, has none, so a call at
    -- one is refused, as a requirement at a structural former with no provider is.
    -- No row of `TypePattern` may be written, so no boundary catches by a forged
    -- `admits`. 027.4 and 071 carry the measurements.
    operation reify[Rho, X, T1](body: () -> X @ {Error[T1], Rho}) -> Result[E = T1, T = X]
      effects {Rho}
      requires TypePattern[T = T1]
  end

  sort Suspension
    provides Effect[T = Suspension]
  end

  sort Branch
    import anthill.prelude.{Nothing}
    provides Effect[T = Branch]
    operation fail() -> Nothing effects Branch
  end

  -- Payload for match-failure errors. Raised through `Error[MatchFailed]` by
  -- the evaluator when a `match` expression has no matching arm. The
  -- exhaustiveness-checked typer removes `Error[MatchFailed]` from a match's
  -- inferred effect row when all arms are covered.
  sort MatchFailed
    import anthill.reflect.{NodeOccurrence, Term}
    entity match_failed(occurrence: NodeOccurrence, scrutinee: Term)
  end

  -- Payload for division-by-zero errors. Carried by `Error[DivisionByZero]` on
  -- the partial integer operations (`Int64.div` / `mod` / `rem` / `divExact`)
  -- and `Field.div` / `Field.recip` (WI-066). Carries `op` — the name of the
  -- operation that divided by zero (e.g. `"Int64.div"`) — so a handler that
  -- catches `Error[DivisionByZero]` can report which op failed. WI-467 routes
  -- the runtime raise through the Error handler (`interp.raise_error`), so an
  -- installed `Error[...]` handler actually catches it; unhandled, it surfaces
  -- as `EvalError::Raised` carrying this payload.
  sort DivisionByZero
    import anthill.prelude.{String}
    entity division_by_zero(op: String)
  end

  -- Payload for head-of-empty errors. Carried by `Error[EmptyStream]` on the
  -- partial `Stream.head` (WI-567) — the ergonomic bare-`T` head, guarded by
  -- `isEmpty(s)`. A nullary marker — the stream is empty by definition, so there
  -- is nothing to carry (cf. `DivisionByZero`). This sort is the TYPE-level
  -- witness the typer threads through callers' rows. NOTE: unlike `DivisionByZero`
  -- (a native builtin the evaluator raises as `EvalError::DivisionByZero`), the
  -- eval-side raise is ordinary surface code (WI-818, delivering the WI-567
  -- follow-on): `Stream.head`/`tail`'s default bodies — and `List.head`'s
  -- override — `Error.raise(empty_stream)` on the empty case, so unhandled it
  -- surfaces as `EvalError::Raised` carrying this payload.
  sort EmptyStream
    entity empty_stream
  end

  -- Payload for a FLOUNDERED relation drain (WI-737). Carried by `Error` on the
  -- Relation face (proposal 052) when materializing an answer that the search
  -- never DECIDED: a solution with a non-empty residual — goals that delayed
  -- waiting on variables which never got bound. `goals` are those undischarged
  -- goals, so a handler can report WHICH goal floundered.
  --
  -- Why this raises rather than yields, and why only here. A `Relation[T]`
  -- promises rows of `T`; `T` has no room for a third "undecided" outcome, so
  -- materializing a floundered answer would either put a logic VARIABLE in a
  -- column the schema types (`Relation[(x: Int64, …)]` yielding `x = ?_1232`) or
  -- read a residual as positive membership. `E ⊇ {Error}` on every relation
  -- already, so this rides a channel the type declares — no signature changes.
  --
  -- THE SPLIT (WI-519): the RAW reflect / LogicalStream face keeps undecidedness
  -- as DATA — `execute` yields `undecided(subst, residual)` and NEVER raises it,
  -- so WI-010's self-hosted resolver can inspect which goals stayed pending. The
  -- TYPED Relation face raises, because a typed row cannot carry that third
  -- outcome. Same fact, two faces; see `Interpreter::make_solution_value` (the
  -- data face) vs `materialize_solution` (this one) in `eval/mod.rs`.
  sort RelationFloundered
    import anthill.prelude.{List}
    import anthill.reflect.{Term}
    entity relation_floundered(goals: List[T = Term])
  end

end

-- Effect relationships:
-- Modify implies the ability to read
-- (Read is not a separate effect kind — it's implied by Modify
--  and operations that only read should take immutable parameters)
